Research decision memo — Stage 3
Title: Ising-Type Social Interactions from Optimal Choices
Status: NOT STARTED

Central hypothesis: Can optimizing households display mean-field/Ising social complementarities while maintaining rational expectations about aggregate states and equilibrium prices?

Feasibility prior:
Conditional: binary actions with complementarities and idiosyncratic taste shocks can generate logistic best responses; do not replace rational forecasts with unvalidated heuristic belief switching.

Core economic distinction:
Same Heterogeneous-Agent GE with peer payoff J=0; separately a non-rational belief-update specification

Conditions to check:
1. Optimal Bellman value differences across actions
2. Logit choice probabilities under stated taste-shock law
3. Mean-field self-consistency m=E[s_i]
4. Rational forecast of aggregate motion conditional on information
5. All-market clearing with endogenous social choices

Pass / fail criterion:
Accept optimal Ising-type strategic complementarity only when the equilibrium mapping, not just a behavioral fixed point, exists.

The project reports negative feasibility results when a kinetic, entropy, adaptive-trading, or heuristic-firm mechanism cannot be reconciled with optimization and equilibrium. Do not silently weaken rational expectations or drop clearing equations.

Evidence status: no derived theorems, numerical solutions or validated simulations committed yet.
